  "articleSummary": "The article argues that agentic cyber threats emerge not from one breakthrough but from multiplying gains in autonomous task duration and multi-agent orchestration, amplified by attackers stealing access so victims pay the compute bill. It cites METR’s task-horizon research, Claude Code’s reported role in the GTG-1002 espionage campaign, and “LLMjacking” through stolen cloud credentials to show how persistent, parallel attacks can become economically asymmetric. Repeated attempts can produce substantial aggregate success even when individual attempts rarely succeed, while human security review remains costly and scales poorly. Aimed at security teams skeptical of agentic threats, the piece is a pointed, darkly humorous threat-model analysis framed through Terminator/Skynet imagery, agents as API calls, swarms, and the refrain that attackers “absolutely will not stop billing.”",
